[{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/comments/2847103310","html_url":"https://github.com/godotengine/godot/issues/105931#issuecomment-2847103310","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/105931","id":2847103310,"node_id":"IC_kwDOAO6SJc6ps1lO","user":{"login":"jrouwe","id":1621693,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE2MjE2OTM=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/1621693?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/jrouwe","html_url":"https://github.com/jrouwe","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jrouwe/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jrouwe/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jrouwe/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jrouwe/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jrouwe/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jrouwe/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jrouwe/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jrouwe/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/jrouwe/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2025-05-02T12:35:06Z","updated_at":"2025-05-02T12:35:06Z","body":"As far as I can see it works correctly if you switch your physics engine to Jolt Physics. When porting Jolt to godot we found that there were many strange corner cases in the godot joints. Note that you'll find that the body rotates in the opposite direction for Jolt because Jolt by default constrains the dynamic body to node B if only 1 node is specified. Godot constrains the dynamic body to node A in that case. The godot behavior causes limits to behave in a strange unintuitive way. You can flip Jolt to behave as godot by setting the following setting:\n\n![Image](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/42177c9c-861b-498e-aa9a-c775352535e3)","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","pin":null,"reaction